            'body_en' => "In the 1930s Max Mallowan sounded the mound of Tell Mozan, judged it Roman, and moved on. It was not Roman at all. When Giorgio Buccellati and Marilyn Kelly-Buccellati began to dig in 1984 they found a city of the third millennium BC — and, on seal impressions from its palace, its name: Urkesh, the city the Hurrian myths had called the home of Kumarbi, father of the gods.\n\nThe Hurrians were a people of the mountains to the north, with their own language unrelated to Sumerian or Akkadian. Urkesh was their first known capital, and the only Hurrian city of its age that has been excavated. The whole site covers about 135 hectares; the high mound, eighteen hectares and twenty-five metres tall, holds the temple and the palace, ringed by a mudbrick wall eight metres wide.\n\nThe excavation has been on hold since 2011. Through the war the site was looked after by its own workers from the villages around it, who kept the mudbrick walls covered and the trenches drained, and showed the place to anyone who came — a way of protecting a ruin that other sites in the region have since copied.",
